// CR claude for claude: [doc-drift/low] The defaults are f64 (0.0 and 1.0). For an
// integer 'a, a call that omits either bound is refused, and the error points at the
// default inside module /rand: `rand(#start: 5, #clock: c)` gives "'a: i64 does not
// contain f64". The draw also fires on any argument, not only #clock, because the args
// are CachedVals, so a change to #start or #end draws a new number. Fix the doc: an
// integer range must give both bounds, and a bound change also draws. An empty range
// produces nothing, which is also worth stating. (gx-stdlib-10)
/// generate a random number between #start and #end (exclusive)
/// every time #clock updates. If start and end are not specified,
/// they default to 0.0 and 1.0
val rand: fn<'a: [Int, Float] + Singleton>(?#start:'a, ?#end:'a, #clock:Any) -> 'a;

/// pick a random element from the array and return it. Update
/// each time the array updates. If the array is empty return
/// nothing.
val pick: fn(a: Array<'a>) -> 'a;

/// return a shuffled copy of a
val shuffle: fn(a: Array<'a>) -> Array<'a>;
